From ca97141e38a3af23ed1782ed00da06ce8e53bed8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 23:02:35 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 114/127] render: a layer named in `dependencies` draws through
 its composite, as WE's flag 0x1010
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The scene load (GH 0x1401b7xxx, in the loader after the parents are linked) gathers the ids in every
object's `dependencies` list and sets image flags 0x1010 on each image or text object among them
(re-running its setup when 0x10 was not set). Flag 0x10 is the "draw off screen, then a last draw"
of a colour-blend or fogged layer (GH 0x140212f90, 0x140214b50): the base and all effects go into
the layer's composite, so `_rt_imageLayerComposite_<id>_a` holds the layer's picture even when it
has no effects.

3354740764's `Clouds` compose layer runs procedural_noise with LAYERED, its 2nd-layer texture
`_rt_imageLayerComposite_51_a`, and depends on 51: a white 4 x 4 solid layer "necessary for layered
clouds". LWE drew 51 straight to the screen and the clouds read an empty composite (0 0 0 0): the
Worley noise's alpha came out a quarter lower, so its dark clouds covered the bottom 30 % of the grass
(−14 … −32 / 255) where WE shows a few. Proved with private copies in WE: the clouds recoloured red
show one patch; with 51 black they move to the high colour; without `dependencies` WE reads a stale
target (blocky); with the slot empty (neutral 0.5) WE and LWE agree.

Such a layer now gets the same last draw (effectpassthrough, BLENDMODE 0) with its base drawn at
the colour (1, 1, 1, 1). A compose (passthrough) layer named as a dependency (3243449890's, read by
clipping masks) keeps LWE's handling. 3354740764: 0.327 → 0.218 against WE (2 s 0.088, 5 s 0.097);
3042865984 (a 5-effect mask layer named by another) unchanged, 0.044.

Origin: ours
Why: docs/player/reports/scene-we-ref.md 3354740764 (the clouds compose layer darkens the grass)
Status: candidate-for-upstream
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
